mcu_timer: advance the timers in closed form

TIMER_Clock() is the largest single cost in the emulator, ahead of
PCM_Update. MCU_Step() calls it after every emulated instruction, so it only
ever advances six timer cycles per call -- there is no long jump to be had
inside one call. The waste is that it walks those cycles applying ticks that
change nothing observable. Instrumenting a run gives the ratio: 55M ticks
produced 1561 events.

So defer instead of jumping. Nothing outside mcu_timer.cpp can see a counter:
mcu_timer_t is private to the file and its only outputs are the tcsr flags,
mcu.interrupt_pending, and TIMER_Read()/TIMER_Read2(). Two new fields track
how far the counters have actually been advanced and the first cycle at which
deferring must stop; every entry point that can observe timer state syncs
first, and the two register-write paths also invalidate the schedule.

Scheduling is the closed form: per timer, the distance to the next observable
thing is the minimum of reaching ocra, reaching ocrb and wrapping, skipping
any whose flag is already latched and accounting for counter-clear-on-match
making some values unreachable. Earliest across all four wins.

Interrupt idempotency was checked rather than assumed. MCU_Interrupt_SetRequest
is a bit set, and MCU_Interrupt_Handle does not clear it on dispatch -- the
lines that would are commented out upstream. Only the register writes clear it,
and those sync first. So a span of ticks collapses to one pass over the final
flags. Idempotent is not the same as invisible, though: a flag latched while
its interrupt is masked must raise the request on the next tick once the enable
is turned on, with no flag change. That is an event too, and missing it is a
bug the test catches.

This supersedes the earlier edge-skipping patch, which only removed the empty
iterations and left the per-edge work. Retired instructions for TIMER_Clock,
on the placeholder-ROM benchmark: 2295M upstream, 2142M with edge-skipping,
38M with this. Whole program 7865M -> 5608M.

Those figures flatter it, because zero-filled ROMs leave the timers quiescent.
patches/tests/timer_closed_form/ includes a microbenchmark that programs the
timers the way firmware would, with an ISR stand-in acknowledging interrupts.
Retired instructions per call there, worst case first: 691 -> 559 -> 184 on the
finest dividers with everything active, and 512 -> 325 -> 25 at realistic
densities. So 3x better than edge-skipping at worst, 10-13x typically.

A variant that recomputes the schedule every call and needs no header change
measured no better than edge-skipping, so the two fields in mcu_timer.h earn
their place. std::popcount for the divider shift turned into a libcall to
__popcountdi2 on both x86-64 and A53; std::countr_zero(mask + 1) is the same
value without one.

Equivalence: the differential test drives both implementations through 9.6M
operations across 480 randomised runs with zero mismatches, and nine
deliberate mutations are all caught. Independently, on the placeholder-ROM
profile every other function in the emulator -- PCM_Update, SM_Update,
MCU_Interrupt_Handle, calc_tv, PCM_ReadROM, MCU_Read, SM_Read -- retires a
bit-identical instruction count, so the emulated MCU executed exactly the same
instruction stream. Real-ROM audio remains unchecked; see patches/README.md.

Found by a subagent; reviewed, re-measured and integrated here.


Supersedes 0001-mcu_timer-skip-uneventful-cycles.patch. Apply this one instead,
not on top of it -- it rewrites the same function starting from upstream.

0001 removed the cycles on which no divider edge falls. What is left is real
work: TIMER_Clock() is called once per emulated instruction and asked to
advance six cycles, so with the /4 divider the three FRTs genuinely tick once
or twice per call, and with the /2 divider three times. Each tick compares the
counter against two compare registers, increments it, tests for overflow and
re-tests three interrupt enables. On a 32-slot workload that was still 27.8% of
retired instructions after 0001, and the profile said 335 instructions per
call.

But almost none of that work produces anything. Between events an FRT counter
only counts, and nothing outside mcu_timer.cpp can see a counter: the struct is
private to this file, and the only ways out of it are the tcsr flags, the
interrupt bitset, and TIMER_Read()/TIMER_Read2(). An instrumented run bears
this out -- 55 million ticks produced 1561 events.

So the timers are advanced lazily. `timer.cycles` still lands exactly where the
original loop left it on every call, but the counters are only brought forward
when a tick could produce something observable. `timer.synced` records how far
they have really been advanced; `timer.next_event` is the first `cycles` value
at which deferring is no longer allowed. Both live in mcu_timer_t, so this
patch touches mcu_timer.h as well -- there is nowhere else to put per-instance
state.

Computing next_event is the closed form. For each timer, work out the number of
ticks until its counter next reads ocra, reads ocrb, or wraps 0xffff -> 0,
allowing for the counter-clear-on-compare-match that makes some values
unreachable; convert ticks to cycles through the divider; take the earliest
across all four timers. Then TIMER_Clock() is four instructions until that
cycle arrives, and every entry point that can observe timer state syncs first.

Three things this depends on, all checked rather than assumed:

  - Interrupt requests are idempotent. MCU_Interrupt_SetRequest(..., 1) is a
    bit set on mcu.interrupt_pending, dispatch in MCU_Interrupt_Handle() does
    not clear the bit (the lines that would are commented out upstream), and
    the only code that clears a timer request is TIMER_Write()/TIMER2_Write(),
    which sync first and clear the matching flag as they do it. That is what
    lets a whole span of ticks collapse into one pass over the final flags.

  - Idempotent is not the same as invisible. If a flag is set while its
    interrupt is masked and the enable is then turned on, upstream raises the
    request on the very next tick although no flag changes. That is an event,
    and TIMER_FrtEventDist()/TIMER_TmrEventDist() return 0 for it.

  - A tmr step mask of 0 means "never step" and is not an edge, in the schedule
    as well as in the advance -- the trap commit 8d6f67a had to fix.

The loop bound is still clamped exactly as 0001 clamped it: timer.cycles
persists across calls, so running past the point the original stopped at would
consume cycles a later, finer divider would have fired on.

std::countr_zero(mask + 1), not std::popcount(mask): both give k for a 2^k - 1
mask, but gcc emits a call to __popcountdi2 unless the target has the
instruction, and neither a Cortex-A53 nor baseline x86-64 does.

Measured on x86-64 against the 32-slot placeholder-ROM benchmark, retired
instructions: whole program 7711M -> 5608M, TIMER_Clock 2142M -> 38M, wall
clock 3.06s -> 1.93s. That workload is flattering -- zero-filled ROMs leave the
timers quiescent after warm-up, 85 syncs in 6.4M calls -- so patches/tests/
also carries a harness that programs the timers the way firmware would and
services the interrupts. There, per call:

              upstream   0001   closed form
  /4 dividers, one periodic FRT, period 512     472    295    14
  /4, three FRTs + 8-bit timer, period 512      512    325    32
  /2, three FRTs + 8-bit timer, period 512      684    552    79
  /2, three FRTs + 8-bit timer, period 64       691    559   184

Worst case measured is 3.0x better than 0001; realistic cases are 7-20x.

patches/tests/timer_closed_form_equivalence.cpp builds the real mcu_timer.cpp
twice -- upstream's and this one -- against a stub mcu.h, and compares
timer.cycles, the whole interrupt bitset and every register read at every
operation over 9.6M randomised operations. patches/tests/timer_mutants.sh
breaks this implementation nine different ways and confirms the test catches
all nine.

Beyond that: in the placeholder-ROM run the per-function instruction counts of
every other function in the emulator -- all the opcode handlers, PCM, the sub
MCU, MCU_Interrupt_Handle -- are bit-identical to upstream's, which means the
emulated MCU executed exactly the same instruction stream. Real-ROM audio is
still the check that matters; see patches/README.md.

